-- statement:
--   This file sets up a framework for constructing weighted bilateral shifts that are quasinilpotent and have vanishing Fuglede-Kadison determinant yet have no nontrivial invariant projections. Scalar tools: negLogCost(t) is +∞ at t=0 and otherwise −log|t|; a real function f has negative-infinite log integral when the integral of negLogCost(f) is ∞, and scalarWeightDeterminant is exp of minus that integral. NormQuasinilpotent(T) means ‖Tⁿ‖^(1/n) → 0. IsContinuousCircleWeight(f) says f on the circle ℝ/ℤ (with Lebesgue probability measure) is continuous, takes values in [0,1], vanishes exactly at 0, and has negative-infinite log integral. On the weighted-shift model, the Hilbert space is ℓ²(ℤ), weights lie in ℓ^∞(ℤ), shift(m) is the unitary index shift, diagonal(w) is the multiplication operator (an algebra homomorphism), operator(w) is shift by 1 composed with diagonal(w), and orbitProduct, translate and tailProjection (onto indices ≥ m) are auxiliary tools; bounded measurable fields of weights give decomposable diagonal operators on L²-valued spaces. Operator-algebra predicates are defined for a von Neumann algebra M: having only trivial invariant projections for T (every projection p in M with (1−p)Tp=0 is 0 or 1), projection atoms, atomic, abelian, diffuse, factor, vector trace ⟨ξ,Aξ⟩, faithful tracial vector, type II₁ factor with a vector, and separable predual. TracialState is a tracial state on a C*-algebra, with Fuglede-Kadison determinant defined as an infimum over ε>0 of exp(τ(log(a*a+ε))/2), logarithmicPotentialDeterminant of a measure, IsBrownMeasure (a probability measure with compact support whose log-potential matches the determinant of a−z), and vector versions of these, together with operatorModulus, polar conjugates, finite and full polar algebras, and ProductPolarStructure (U unitary, T=U|T|, |T| injective with countably atomic spectral type, finite polar algebras abelian and atomic, full one abelian and diffuse). Two models are built. The rotation model uses L²(circle; ℓ²(ℤ)) with the rotation x ↦ x+kθ, the unitary U, the multiplication V from the character e^{2πix}, the von Neumann algebra generated by U and V, the weighted operator U·(orbit multiplication by a), and the vacuum vector with vacuum functional. Rotation.IsContinuousCounterexample(θ,f) states that f is a continuous circle weight and the weighted operator lies in this algebra, has only trivial invariant projections, is nonzero, quasinilpotent, and has zero vacuum Fuglede-Kadison determinant. The product model uses base space Circle^ℕ with infinite product Lebesgue measure, coordinatewise rotation by angles θ, a logarithmic cost sum of c_i times a floor term, the weight exp(−cost), the shift and multipliers, the generated factor, and trace vector. Admissible angles and costs require 0<θᵢ<1, cᵢ>0, summable θ, and rational independence of 1 and the θᵢ. Further definitions describe triangular laws on endpoint pairs, weak convergence, orbit-pair supported measures with uniformly bounded L² marginals, finite-coordinate sampling, covariant field representations, and a central diagonal integral. HasCounterexampleOperator(θ,c) packages the product shift-times-weight operator as nonzero, quasinilpotent, with zero determinant, spectrum {0}, Dirac Brown measure at 0, trivial invariant projections, a type II₁ factor with separable predual, and polar structure. IsCounterexampleRealization combines admissibility, sampling, a.e. positive weight, negative-infinite log integral of the weight, and HasCounterexampleOperator. These are defined propositions only; the Results namespace is empty and no existence theorem is proved.
